PMB OS: file name lookups without reading whole directories (Android storage).

Windows file names are case-insensitive. On a case-sensitive file system Wine therefore reads the whole directory
whenever a name is not found exactly as spelled - for every file that is being created and for every DLL, module or
config file a program merely probes for. On Android's shared storage (/storage/emulated: a FUSE file system served by
the MediaProvider daemon, about 0.2 ms per directory entry) that made creating files in a directory of 1000 run at
6 files per second and a Ren'Py game take over a minute to start; yet that file system matches names in any case by
itself, so the search is pointless there.

- FUSE file systems are asked once (an existing entry is looked up under a name with the other case) and the answer
  is remembered per file system: no directory search on the case-insensitive ones, and no EXT2_IOC_GETFLAGS ioctl on
  FUSE (Android's SELinux policy denies and logs it).
- The short (8.3) name search runs only for names that can be Wine's own hashed short names ("ABCD~XYZ.EXT"). It used
  to run for every name that merely fits 8.3 ("site.py"), which defeats the shortcut for case-insensitive file systems
  (also ext4 / f2fs casefold directories, which PMB OS uses for the Windows prefix).
- The VFAT_IOCTL_READDIR_BOTH ioctl is no longer tried on every directory that is listed or searched: there are no
  VFAT volumes here, and each attempt on shared storage is denied and written to the audit log.

